@page "/privacy"
@{
    Layout = "/Pages/Shared/_PublicLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy policy";
    const string contactEmail = "dmitry.shapar@gmail.com";
}

<h1>Privacy policy</h1>
<p class="updated">Last updated: October 1, 2026</p>
<p>This policy describes the DeyeSolar mobile app and the Solar Energy Management service at
    <a href="https://solar.dshapar.com">solar.dshapar.com</a>. Contact the service operator at
    <a href="mailto:@contactEmail">@contactEmail</a> with privacy questions.</p>

<h2>Information used by the service</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Account and sign-in information:</strong> usernames, verified email addresses and phone numbers, linked Google account identifiers,
        password authentication records, verification challenges, and session tokens used to sign in.</li>
    <li><strong>Configuration you save:</strong> Deye and Shelly connection settings, including account email,
        credentials, API secrets, station and device identifiers, and polling preferences.</li>
    <li><strong>Installation information:</strong> inverter and battery readings, solar generation estimates and
        history, device status, automation rules you create, and records of rule execution. The service also
        stores export readings and electricity settlement data to calculate estimated sales.</li>
</ul>
<p>The mobile app stores its selected server address locally. On iOS and Android, it uses the operating
    system's secure storage for the sign-in session. The mobile app does not save the password entered
    on its sign-in screen.</p>

<h2>How information is used</h2>
<p>The service uses this information to authenticate users, connect to configured energy and device
    services, display readings and estimates, control devices, and run automation rules. Saved connection
    credentials are held by the server so it can communicate with those services.</p>
<p>Each new account has its own installation storage, credentials, devices, settings and history.
    Users with membership in the same installation can access that installation. Existing accounts retain
    their access to the installation migrated from the previous shared service.</p>

<h2>Connected services</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Google:</strong> when you choose Google sign-in or linking, Google verifies your identity
        and supplies its account identifier and verified email address. The service does not receive your Google password.</li>
    <li><strong>Resend:</strong> when email verification is enabled, your email address and verification message
        are sent to Resend for delivery.</li>
    <li><strong>Twilio Verify:</strong> when phone verification is enabled, your phone number and verification
        request are sent to Twilio to deliver and verify the SMS code.</li>
    <li><strong>Deye Cloud:</strong> the server uses configured Deye account and application credentials and
        station or device identifiers to request inverter and energy information.</li>
    <li><strong>Shelly Cloud:</strong> the server uses the configured authorization key and device identifiers
        to retrieve device status and send device commands.</li>
    <li><strong>Open-Meteo:</strong> the server sends the configured solar installation coordinates to retrieve
        weather and solar radiation information. The mobile app does not request the phone's GPS location.</li>
</ul>
<p>The web dashboard loads fonts from Google Fonts, which involves a direct browser request to Google.
    Service requests also pass through the hosting and network providers used to operate the server.
    These providers may process technical connection information necessary to deliver the service.</p>
<p>The mobile app does not include advertising or analytics SDKs and does not use data for cross-app
    advertising tracking.</p>

<h2>App Store subscriptions</h2>
<p>Apple processes subscription payments through the App Store. The iOS app uses StoreKit to verify
    subscription status locally on the device. This status includes subscription product and transaction
    identifiers and entitlement information. The current app does not send these subscription records to
    the DeyeSolar server. DeyeSolar does not receive your payment card or bank account details.</p>

<h2>Storage and retention</h2>
<p>Account records, saved connection settings, automation rules, and export or settlement history do not
    have an automatic expiry configured. They remain on the server until changed or removed by the
    operator. Operational readings and rule execution records are subject to periodic server cleanup.</p>
<p>Verification challenges expire after ten minutes; mobile Google tickets expire after two minutes.
    Mobile sessions expire and can be revoked. Signing out clears the stored mobile session and attempts
    to revoke that session on the server. Signing out does not delete the server account, connection
    settings, or installation history.</p>

<h2>Your choices and requests</h2>
<p>You can sign out and change the configured server in the mobile app. You can edit connection settings
    and automation rules when signed in to a server you administer. To request access, correction, or
    deletion of information held by this service, or to stop its processing, contact
    <a href="mailto:@contactEmail">@contactEmail</a>. Describe your request and the relevant account or
    installation without sending passwords or API secrets. The operator may need to verify that you
    are authorized to make the request.</p>
<p>If you connect the mobile app to a different server, that server's operator is responsible for its data
    handling. Ask that operator for its privacy policy and data requests.</p>

<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
<p>This page will be updated when the service's data handling changes. The date above identifies the
    latest update.</p>
